# Why do some people dislike using text messages?
I think there are two main reasons why some people dislike using text messages. First of all, it's easy to forget about important messages. Nowadays, people use many different messaging apps for both work and personal communication, so it's easy to overlook or miss some important texts. Second, text messages often lack emotion and nonverbal cues, such as facial expressions or tone of voice, which can lead to misunderstandings. So I think these two factors—forgetting important messages and the lack of emotional cues—are the main reasons why some people dislike using text messages.

# Have the ways that people communicated with each other changed much in the last few decades?
I think the way people communicate with each other has changed significantly over the last few decades. In the past, people mainly relied on face-to-face conversations or written letters to stay in touch. Nowadays, thanks to technological advancements, many people use messaging apps and social media platforms, which make communication much more convenient and efficient. We can now communicate with people almost instantly, regardless of where they are. So, I think technology has completely transformed the way we interact with each other.

# Why do people prefer texting as compared to phone calls?
I think people often prefer texting to making phone calls because it’s more convenient and flexible. With texting, people can reply whenever they have time, so they don’t have to stop what they’re doing to answer a call. It’s also less intrusive because a text message doesn’t require an immediate response. Another advantage is that you have a written record of the conversation, so you can easily go back and check the details later. I think texting is especially suitable for short and simple conversations, while phone calls are better for more complicated or personal matters.

# Do you think modern technology has any negative influence on communication?
I think modern technology has both positive and negative effects on communication. On the one hand, it allows us to communicate quickly over long distances and makes communication much more convenient and accessible. On the other hand, it can also cause problems, such as misunderstandings due to the lack of emotional cues, less face-to-face interaction, and weaker social skills. So, I think striking a good balance between using digital tools and spending time with people in person is essential for maintaining healthy communication.

# What do you think are some of the main differences between written communication and spoken communication?
I think there are several differences between written and spoken communication. First of all, written communication gives people more time to think about what they want to say, so they can organize their ideas and edit their words before sending a message. In contrast, spoken communication is more immediate, so people often have to respond on the spot. Another major difference is that when we speak, we can use our tone of voice, facial expressions, and body language to express emotions, whereas these cues are often missing in written communication. I also think spoken communication tends to be more casual, while written communication can be more formal and detailed, especially in professional or academic contexts.
